WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.250
So who's my guest this week?

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:03.829
Dr. Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:08.660
Harvard, MIT, ex Finance Minister,

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.760
so far so predictable.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.010
But this head of the
World Trade Organization

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.200
is also breaking down barriers.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.300
The General Council agree to appoint

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.466
Dr. Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala of Nigeria

00:00:20.466 --> 00:00:25.182
as the next Director General of the WTO.

00:00:25.182 --> 00:00:27.770
The first woman, the first African,

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:31.030
and wanting to inject
morality in how we trade.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.160
She's fought corruption
and put her own safety

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:34.463
at risk by doing so.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.400
She's been named one of
the most powerful women

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.333
in the world, four years running.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.290
The World Trade Organization.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:45.907
It made China. China made
themselves, they did a good job.

00:00:45.907 --> 00:00:49.080
But they ripped off our country for years.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.860
But can she counter
accusations that the WTO

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.833
is in hock to China and
embeds global inequality?

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.020
I've got a feeling you're going
to like this conversation.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.270
Here's a taste.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.680
The goal I have is to
make everyone understand,

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.930
including the members themselves sometimes

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.597
when they forget that
we are here for people.

00:01:09.597 --> 00:01:12.482
And when you say people
don't understand the WTO,

00:01:12.482 --> 00:01:15.750
do you think the WTO has to
take some of the fault for that?

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.220
Well, you're right. I agree with that.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:20.750
It is the fault that
people don't understand

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.700
really what it is and what it does.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.530
Has being a woman helped or hindered

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:26.202
in your political life?

00:01:26.202 --> 00:01:29.460
I think it's helped

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.020
in the sense that I'm happy to be a woman.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.423
I'm proud of being a woman.
I'm proud of being who I am.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.130
Dr. Ngozi, thank you for joining me.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:55.688
You have just reached a
year at the helm of the WTO.

00:01:55.688 --> 00:01:59.100
I believe you threatened
to walk out last September,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.020
or you could do, you
hadn't bought furniture

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.630
in your place in Geneva yet.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.657
What changed?

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:09.150
I think that there was too
much made of what I was saying.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.374
I didn't threaten to walk out.

00:02:11.374 --> 00:02:15.378
I told members, indeed I
had not bought furniture,

00:02:15.378 --> 00:02:20.378
to spur them on, to try and
negotiate harder, work harder.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.970
For me, this is a job I'm
really passionate about.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:27.260
So even when I make comments,

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.870
it's all geared to getting more

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.730
of the work done, getting results.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.050
If you're passionate about
something and you feel

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.760
that it's, you're there
to deliver for people.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.650
So I'm impatient for action, for results.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:44.250
That's what drives me.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.410
Yes, I suppose it was just, if you felt

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.030
that the WTO had done a better job

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.800
at getting, if you like,
more respected by those

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.440
it seems to have lost respect
amongst, not least America.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:00.290
Well, I don't agree with
the way you characterize it.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.820
It's amazing that even when
you say the organization

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:07.050
has lost respect or is not supported,

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:08.480
one way you can gauge that

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.070
is whether members pay their dues.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.450
If you've lost respect for an organization

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.800
and you no longer want to be part of it,

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.800
you wouldn't support it, would you?

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.590
But I'm very proud to say that
including the United States,

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:25.950
all our members are paying up their dues,

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.010
they're very supportive.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:31.570
Yes, we do have some
issues, some challenges.

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:32.650
Joe Biden has just said,

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:35.490
the US continues to
have systematic concerns

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.080
with the WTO and at the heart of this,

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.128
for those who aren't
that familiar with it,

00:03:40.128 --> 00:03:42.740
there's this concern about the requirement

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:44.660
for American workers to compete

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:48.830
against Chinese forced labor,
but not stopping sometimes

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:52.600
the theft of American intellectual
property and products.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.430
It's about how you have made
the playing field unequal.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:03:58.910
That's one of the biggest criticisms

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:03.910
that actually Trump, Obama,
and Biden have agreement on.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:05.970
I think there's a misnomer here.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:09.810
The WTO does not make the
playing field unequal.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.828
The role is to level the
playing field and to put

00:04:12.828 --> 00:04:17.120
rules in place that will ensure
fair trade for everybody.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:21.280
Now, have those rules been
changed in the past 25 years?

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:22.130
The answer is no.

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.793
So obviously some of the agreements

00:04:24.793 --> 00:04:26.610
need to be brought up to date.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.250
Some of the rules have to be looked at,

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.630
and new rules have to be put in place.

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:34.280
So that is what is the issue here,

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.390
but to say that the WTO doesn't serve,

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:41.610
I don't agree with that
because 75% of world trade

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:45.080
still takes place based on WTO rules

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.960
and in spite of the hot rhetoric
you hear between countries,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.614
let's say US and China, trade
between the two countries

00:04:51.614 --> 00:04:53.530
is still quite robust.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:55.670
No, but it's, I'm going off the signs

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.580
from the leaders of those
countries who actually

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:59.230
have very different political views

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:01.740
but they've all expressed frustration.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:05.670
And you yourself have just
said, those rules need updating.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.850
So what's the WTO been doing?

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:09.550
What has the WTO been doing?

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:13.050
The WTO has been maintaining
that level playing field

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:15.090
for most of the trade in the world.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:17.450
As I said, in spite of all the criticism,

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:21.393
75% of world trade still
takes place on the basis

00:05:21.393 --> 00:05:24.850
of those rules and if it
didn't exist, like I said

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.990
when I was competing for this
job, you'd have to invent it.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:31.200
So the key issue is how do we make

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:32.658
the reforms that are needed?

00:05:32.658 --> 00:05:34.030
Yes, that is my question.

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:35.440
That, sorry, that is at the heart.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:36.920
No, absolutely.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:39.350
To point out that there are
challenges, there are reforms,

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:43.440
I myself said that when I
was competing for this job.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.210
So we need to embark on those reforms.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:49.860
And indeed we are beginning to do that.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:54.170
Part of the reform is to complete
multilateral negotiations,

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:57.210
which the organization has
not done in a long time.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:58.990
And that's what we've been pushing for.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:01.500
We are nearer closer than we've ever been

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.000
to completing the fishery
subsidies negotiations.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:08.965
And I think had it not been
the fact that we postponed

00:06:08.965 --> 00:06:13.832
our ministerial meeting from last November

00:06:13.832 --> 00:06:15.030
due to Omicron,

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.450
we could have been there by now.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.952
So we are regaining momentum,

00:06:18.952 --> 00:06:21.327
to try to complete that.

00:06:21.327 --> 00:06:25.230
We are also working on the
response to the pandemic.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:28.280
The WTO has been responsive to an issue

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:31.530
of the global commons
that is facing us now.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:35.030
We've been working very hard
on several facets of this.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:38.100
We've been working on supply chain issues

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:40.150
with vaccine manufacturers.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:43.980
This is where we have a monitoring
and transparency function

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.898
that can help producers
look at what is happening

00:06:46.898 --> 00:06:48.857
to the supply chains.

00:06:48.857 --> 00:06:51.028
So this is very important.

00:06:51.028 --> 00:06:54.950
You have huge history and experience

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:57.570
having been chair of
Gavi, the Vaccine Alliance

00:06:57.570 --> 00:07:01.830
for a major moment for
the WTO to perhaps improve

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.370
the view of it in the eyes of its critics.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:07.790
Where is the WTO going to come
down on the side of patents

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:10.840
with regards to vaccines
and intellectual property

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:12.960
or lifting them for what's described

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.050
as vaccine equity around the world,

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:17.210
because I'm unclear having read into this.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:19.388
Well, first off all,
let me start by saying

00:07:19.388 --> 00:07:22.950
that vaccine inequity is plain wrong.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.550
And we've, I've been very clear on that

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:28.210
as the Director General of the WTO.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:31.650
Inequity in access doesn't serve anybody,

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:33.510
even those who have been vaccinated,

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:37.790
because it could lead to
more variants multiplying.

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:41.243
Now, the WTO, as I came in, I realized

00:07:41.243 --> 00:07:43.330
that several of the functions there

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:45.249
could really be useful in helping

00:07:45.249 --> 00:07:48.610
the world to get to a
more equitable situation

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.440
with respect to vaccines.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:53.510
Like I said, people take it for granted,

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:57.500
but the supply chains of
vaccines are very complicated

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:00.890
because they have so many
components, up to 280,

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:03.181
for some of the MRNA vaccines.

00:08:03.181 --> 00:08:06.980
If you have an export
restriction of prohibition

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:08.190
on one of those products,

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:10.380
it means the vaccine cannot be produced.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:13.680
So simply getting our members to reduce

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:16.940
the number of export
restrictions and prohibitions,

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.830
which were 119 at the
start of the pandemic,

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.800
and we are now down to 35 has helped

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.990
to boost production in the world,

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:28.020
helping the producers
monitor the bottlenecks

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.660
in their supply chain, raw materials,

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:34.000
and so on has helped them
know what to do with that.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.260
So these kinds of functions
are very important.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:38.570
Trade facilitation.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:41.020
We have a trade facilitation agreement

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:43.110
designed to make goods move faster.

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:46.000
And this has been very
helpful to the manufacturers.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.600
We are meeting with the CEOs
of the vaccine manufacturers,

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:53.160
the 10 of them every few
weeks to go over the issue.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:55.360
That's one side that is often overlooked

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:58.720
because people focus
on the IP and patents,

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.825
but to make vaccines, you
need a smooth supply chain.

00:09:01.825 --> 00:09:04.190
You need manufacturing capacity,

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:07.210
which we've also been working
with the manufacturers on,

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:09.950
and then you need
technology transfer and IP.

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:11.752
On the IP side, there's been

00:09:11.752 --> 00:09:15.620
negotiations as you know at the WTO

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:17.900
that were going on for almost a year

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.650
before I came on the scene.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:22.100
And it's been very
difficult because of course

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:24.230
there are two polarized sides.

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:26.310
Proponents - more than a hundred countries

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.320
who want a waiver of the vaccine,

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.150
seeing it as being- of the
IP, intellectual property,

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:34.860
seeing it as material to
manufacturing vaccines,

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:38.170
and those who believe that
this could be a detriment

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:40.810
to innovation and to
research and development,

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:41.840
the non-proponents.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.510
Now, what we've tried to do

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:46.080
is to bridge the gap between the two.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:47.240
We're still working on it.

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:50.530
We're not there yet, but
we're inching forward.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:53.810
And we have a process going on now.

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:55.335
How you do that when you say

00:09:55.335 --> 00:09:58.400
it's two polarized sides like that?

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:00.310
When you talk about bridging the gap,

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:01.570
you are the Director General.

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:03.500
How do you go about doing that?

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:06.030
And do you see an answer soon?

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:08.490
Well, actually the way things work here,

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:10.620
members are supposed to talk to each other

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:12.010
and try to bridge their gaps.

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:14.580
But I've stepped into the process

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:16.650
to try to help that along.

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:19.130
And it's well known, so I can say it,

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:22.090
We're trying to work with
four countries at the moment,

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:25.810
the US, the EU, India, and South Africa

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:28.840
to see if we can come to a framework

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:30.010
which we can then share

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:32.920
with the rest of the
membership fairly quickly

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:35.626
so we can get to agreement on a text

00:10:35.626 --> 00:10:38.400
that everybody can support.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:42.580
It'll be a compromise solution
between the two sides.

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:45.080
So by its nature, a compromise

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:47.600
will not be satisfactory to all sides,

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.641
but it is, there's what
we call a landing zone.

00:10:50.641 --> 00:10:54.180
It'll be a workable compromise,

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:56.040
and that's what we are aiming for.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:59.090
What could that look like in the sense of,

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:01.067
will it be that certain vaccines

00:11:01.067 --> 00:11:04.300
that the patent is
waived and others aren't?

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:05.650
No, absolutely not.

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:07.200
And I'm afraid, Emma,

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.410
that this is one of
the things I cannot do.

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:11.670
We are in the middle
of these negotiations.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:15.650
So just talking to you about
it, puts it in jeopardy.

00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:17.752
So we have to be very sensible.

00:11:17.752 --> 00:11:21.410
I cannot go into the details
of what it could look like.

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:24.150
When the agreement is
made, we can talk about it.

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.840
I've got to try and get
as much as I can though,

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:30.265
to understand, I suppose,
from your perspective,

00:11:30.265 --> 00:11:34.970
how important is this particular
landing zone to get right,

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:38.130
in terms of whether the
WTO should exist at all?

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:40.610
Because there are some
who really don't think

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:42.460
it is fit in its current state.

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:44.850
Well, the WTO, I don't think

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:47.870
we should mix up all sorts of issues.

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:51.650
I've already made it clear
that the WTO is playing a role

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:54.450
that people don't appreciate in the world.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:56.900
If it didn't exist, there
would really be trouble

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:59.050
in the multilateral trading system.

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:02.820
That's not to say that the
WTO does not have challenges

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:05.330
and has to be made fit for purpose,

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:08.722
but this issue of the IP waiver,

00:12:08.722 --> 00:12:12.585
there is a suitable
compromise that would allow

00:12:12.585 --> 00:12:17.585
those who want access to be able to meet

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:20.420
some of their needs whilst making sure

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:24.290
that we don't disincentivize
research and innovation,

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:26.920
which is what the non-proponents want.

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:30.030
So that's the kind of middle
ground we're looking for.

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:33.120
So nobody will have
100% of what they want,

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:35.210
but there'll be a workable solution

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:36.820
that both sides can live with.

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:38.914
And the workable solution
to, I don't know,

00:12:38.914 --> 00:12:41.543
a farmer in America, who's lost his job

00:12:41.543 --> 00:12:44.870
because of unfair competition with China.

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:47.060
What's a workable solution that the WTO

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:48.850
comes for him or her?

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:51.030
If they're thinking well,
I'm not sure America

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:52.805
should pay their dues to an organization

00:12:52.805 --> 00:12:55.450
that has still failed to reform

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:58.640
trading practices between
two giant members?

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:00.816
Let me repeat one thing.

00:13:00.816 --> 00:13:04.620
WTO is a member-driven organization.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.620
So the members need to talk
to each other and negotiate.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:10.236
It provides a negotiating forum.

00:13:10.236 --> 00:13:14.940
So for them to approach each
other, talk to each other,

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:16.770
to try and resolve these issues.

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:19.700
That being said, there
are certain agreements

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:22.430
that have been in place at the WTO,

00:13:22.430 --> 00:13:25.830
which if we renew or revamp could help

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:27.070
with some of these issues.

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:29.885
But first we have to get our facts right.

00:13:29.885 --> 00:13:31.885
Because a farmer has,

00:13:31.885 --> 00:13:35.810
or somebody working in a
coal mine has lost their job.

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:38.599
We really need to look at
the factors behind that.

00:13:38.599 --> 00:13:42.450
It's sometimes lazy thinking
to just blame trade.

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:44.570
Sometimes it's technology

00:13:44.570 --> 00:13:47.400
that is the cause of that displacement.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:49.430
And you need governments to implement

00:13:49.430 --> 00:13:52.090
active labor market policies to solve it.

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:53.900
Sorry, but if I may,

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:57.070
political leaders and I named
three American presidents

00:13:57.070 --> 00:14:00.036
who are not aligned always,
it's fair to say politically,

00:14:00.036 --> 00:14:03.240
have all said, if I say
the Trump Administration

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:06.020
accused the WTO of being
completely inadequate

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:10.250
and Joe Biden to repeat says
the US has systematic concerns.

00:14:10.250 --> 00:14:12.760
And we've also seen you
talk about paying your dues,

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:15.060
that's one indicator if a country's happy,

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:17.510
but it's now an not going
to, the Biden Administration

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:19.570
is not going to appoint
new members to the panel

00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:22.300
who look at trade disputes with the WTO.

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:24.590
What I'm trying to say is
there is a narrative here

00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:27.360
where in one hand you say,
it's a member organization,

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:28.830
they've got to talk to each other,

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:31.690
but you are receiving millions
and millions of pounds

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:35.190
and dollars to help countries do

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:36.930
what they can't do their own.

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:39.260
So you can't say it's up to countries

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:41.370
and then still have this arbitration role.

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:45.583
No, you, I think people
don't understand the WTO.

00:14:45.583 --> 00:14:47.921
The WTO is a member organization

00:14:47.921 --> 00:14:50.962
that has a secretariat
that supports members

00:14:50.962 --> 00:14:53.630
to get these negotiations done.

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:57.690
So there is no kind of
WTO sitting out there,

00:14:57.690 --> 00:14:59.240
it's the members are the WTO.

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:00.980
Hang on, sorry, are you saying Joe Biden

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:03.330
doesn't understand the WTO
'cause I'm taking my lead

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:05.420
from the president of America.

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:08.720
No, I'm saying that the way
you're characterizing it

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:10.080
is not the way it is.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:13.640
Let tell you what people
are frustrated at.

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:16.810
If members have not been
able to talk to each other

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:18.480
with the support of the secretariat

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:20.570
to solve some of these problems,

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:22.530
of course there's frustration.

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:26.600
And it is true that the US,

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:28.170
said they don't like what goes on

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:30.400
with the dispute settlement system.

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:33.650
And that the way the system
runs the appellate body

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:37.380
has overreached in terms of
the way it makes judgments.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:40.380
That has been a complaint
over many administrations.

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:42.200
And what's the answer to that?

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:44.370
The answer is that we should reform it,

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:46.630
which is what we're presently discussing.

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:48.650
One of the things we want to make sure

00:15:48.650 --> 00:15:50.720
we table at our 12th Ministerial,

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:53.010
which is now going to be on June 13,

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:55.440
is how do we indeed reform,

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:58.970
do the fundamental reforms of the WTO?

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:03.610
How do we rework the
dispute settlement mechanism

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:07.040
so that it can serve all the
members the way they want?

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:10.200
If I may there is presently
a dispute in the WTO

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:13.260
amongst the members about
how to handle disputes?

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:14.210
Yes.

00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:15.043
Right.

00:16:15.043 --> 00:16:16.965
Okay, and when you say-
That is true,

00:16:16.965 --> 00:16:19.460
When you say people
don't understand the WTO,

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:22.360
do you think the WTO has to
take some of the fault for that?

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:25.360
That it's this organization
that isn't well understood

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:26.601
around the world and people don't think

00:16:26.601 --> 00:16:29.150
it provides value for money sometimes?

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:31.940
Well you are right. I agree with that.

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:34.410
And that is, I think it is the fault

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:36.260
that people don't understand really

00:16:36.260 --> 00:16:38.200
what it is and what it does.

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:40.110
And it's mischaracterized.

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:42.140
Including the president of America?

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:45.150
I'm not saying that, I don't
want words put in my mouth.

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:47.850
I think the president is not satisfied

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:50.240
with the way the organization is working

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:51.350
because they're not happy

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:53.310
with the dispute settlement mechanism,

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:56.320
and some of their
complaints are well founded.

00:16:56.320 --> 00:16:59.890
That's why we need to reform
the dispute settlement system.

00:16:59.890 --> 00:17:03.000
But to reform it is those same members

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:04.800
that have to sit around the table,

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:08.020
there is no rescuer coming from outside.

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:10.030
You've not got an easy job, have you?

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:10.863
You really don't-

00:17:10.863 --> 00:17:14.400
The US and all the others
have to sit around the table

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:18.240
and agree on how they're
going to make these reforms,

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:22.028
be it dispute settlement, be
it the monitoring function.

00:17:22.028 --> 00:17:25.180
Now, all I can tell you is that as the DG,

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:27.240
we try to support them to do that.

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:30.330
And now we are looking at the reform

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:34.280
of the WTO's core functions
as one of the big subjects

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:39.280
that we're going to look
at at the 12th Ministerial.

00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:42.130
So we will start the discussion
and the debate there,

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:43.636
and then they will agree eventually

00:17:43.636 --> 00:17:45.730
on how they want to move this forward.

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:47.330
What gets you out of bed in the morning?

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:48.340
What motivates you?

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:49.380
You could have been having-

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:50.605
I think I have one of the most

00:17:50.605 --> 00:17:54.110
interesting jobs in the world,
that's why I applied for it.

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:57.820
Let me tell you something, this WTO,

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:01.530
the purpose of this was
inscribed in the charter

00:18:01.530 --> 00:18:03.990
when it was created in 1994

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:08.414
in the preamble to the
Marrakesh Agreement.

00:18:08.414 --> 00:18:11.700
And it says that the WTO's purpose

00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:14.250
is to enhance living standards,

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:17.160
to help create employment and to support

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:18.860
sustainable development.

00:18:18.860 --> 00:18:21.530
In other words, it's all about people,

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:22.950
and that's what drives me.

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:26.520
And the goal I have is to
make everyone understand,

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:28.280
including the members themselves,

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:32.040
sometimes when they forget
that we are here for people.

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:32.873
You are right.

00:18:32.873 --> 00:18:36.310
The ordinary person on the
street in my village in Nigeria

00:18:36.310 --> 00:18:38.827
needs to know why the WTO exists

00:18:38.827 --> 00:18:40.870
and what it exists for, because it's doing

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:43.570
some very, very important and good things.

00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:45.090
So that's what drives me.

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:48.570
I get up in the morning,
ready, raring to go to work

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:50.740
because I want us to deliver for people.

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:54.550
The Fisheries Subsidies
Agreement, let me just take that.

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:57.510
There are 260 million people that depend

00:18:57.510 --> 00:18:58.930
on fisheries in the world.

00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:01.570
If we're able to conclude that deal,

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:04.260
we will stop overcapacity and overfishing,

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:06.680
which is now almost at 50%.

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:09.934
And that will secure the
livelihoods of so many people.

00:19:09.934 --> 00:19:12.950
When we get to the
response to this pandemic,

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:15.720
and we have a package
agreed by all members,

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:18.774
we have been contributing
already very materially

00:19:18.774 --> 00:19:23.350
to solving the issue of
vaccines, production, and access,

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:24.989
which I'm very proud about.

00:19:24.989 --> 00:19:29.230
And that is something
that delivers for people.

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:31.520
So that's what I want to everyone to know.

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:34.040
The WTO is about people.

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:36.850
You have been credited in your time

00:19:36.850 --> 00:19:39.860
in politics in Nigeria,
which you've just mentioned

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:41.950
with saving a lot of
money for the country.

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:44.130
You're in fact nicknamed
the $9 billion woman

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:46.970
for making sure that you
could try and face down

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:48.820
as much corruption as possible.

00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:51.920
What is it about you and
your style, do you think,

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:54.120
that you hope helps people get

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:57.360
to more agreement, to a better place?

00:19:57.360 --> 00:20:00.393
Well, I think it's
because I never give up.

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:04.050
I'm an optimist by nature.

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:06.400
I am very enthus-

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:09.990
I never take on a job unless
I'm passionate about it.

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:12.360
So I get up every morning wanting to go,

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:14.180
and I think people see that.

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:17.890
They also see that no matter
how difficult the situation is,

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:19.090
I will not give up.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:22.560
I will work as hard as
possible to push for results.

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:24.550
I'm very results-oriented

00:20:24.550 --> 00:20:27.270
and that's what is attractive at the WTO.

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:30.900
We can achieve results
that will benefit people.

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:32.230
So people see that,

00:20:32.230 --> 00:20:34.900
and I think they can
relate to the sincerity

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:36.130
that I have for the job.

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.160
Has being a woman helped or hindered

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:39.396
in your political life?

00:20:41.410 --> 00:20:43.500
I think it's helped in the sense

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:45.450
that I'm happy to be a woman.

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:47.030
I'm proud of being a woman.

00:20:47.030 --> 00:20:49.990
I'm proud of being who
I am from where I am.

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:53.810
So when you have that self
confidence, it helps you to lead.

00:20:53.810 --> 00:20:55.510
Of course, when you are a woman

00:20:55.510 --> 00:20:58.740
and I've written a book
about it with Julia Gillard,

00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:01.260
the former Prime Minister of Australia,

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:03.210
which is quite popular.

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:05.270
Now it's called Women and Leadership:

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:07.010
Real Lives, Real Lessons.

00:21:07.010 --> 00:21:10.590
And we look at eight women
leaders and at ourselves,

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:13.380
you will see that when
you're a woman in leadership,

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:16.267
there are a number of challenges

00:21:16.267 --> 00:21:18.940
that confront you along the way

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:21.330
from looking at how you handle issues.

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:24.010
You cannot be too soft because people

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:26.180
will then criticize
you for being too soft,

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:28.110
but you can't be too strident

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:30.572
because then they think
you're very aggressive.

00:21:30.572 --> 00:21:34.650
So women leaders we found are
always watching their style

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:37.000
to make sure that they balance it out.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:38.230
But at the end of the day,

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:41.750
what most of the women
in leadership have found

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:44.100
is that you just have to be yourself.

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:46.890
You lead best when you are yourself,

00:21:46.890 --> 00:21:48.200
because you're authentic

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:50.600
and you don't have to
try and be anyone else.

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:53.080
So of course there've been
challenges along the way.

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:54.660
If you're a woman of color,

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:57.766
there are even more
challenges along the way.

00:21:57.766 --> 00:22:00.620
When I walk into a room,
people are surprised

00:22:00.620 --> 00:22:02.330
when they see the way I'm dressed,

00:22:02.330 --> 00:22:03.960
which is the way I've always dressed.

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:06.270
And you can see some assumptions

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:09.445
on the faces of some of the people,

00:22:09.445 --> 00:22:11.730
because they look at your appearance

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:12.950
and make some judgements.

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:17.700
Maybe this person cannot
handle the job or whatever,

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:21.160
but the minute I open my mouth
and talk about the substance,

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:22.600
people forget that.

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:26.110
So I don't spend time dwelling
on whether I'm a woman

00:22:26.110 --> 00:22:27.370
or a woman of color.

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:29.960
I dwell on what do I bring to the job

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:32.450
and how is my knowledge helping

00:22:32.450 --> 00:22:34.180
to change things in the place?

00:22:34.180 --> 00:22:35.770
And that's what excites me.

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:38.347
I think when people see you
have what it takes to run,

00:22:38.347 --> 00:22:40.480
they relate to it.

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:42.610
I've been a manager for instance,

00:22:42.610 --> 00:22:46.180
for a long time in my
career at The World Bank,

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:47.810
which is a huge organization.

00:22:47.810 --> 00:22:51.140
I've been Minister of Finance
with thousands of people

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:54.420
under my, in my ministry.

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:56.290
So I have experience.

00:22:56.290 --> 00:22:58.720
So when I come to a job at the WTO,

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:00.850
I try to put that experience

00:23:00.850 --> 00:23:03.280
to the benefit of the organization.

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:07.610
And what I can say is I'm,

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:08.880
I love the job.

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:12.410
I have to be very honest,
but I also have to be honest

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:13.631
that I do get irritated

00:23:13.631 --> 00:23:16.770
when we don't move
quickly towards results.

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:19.263
Maybe that's what, the only aspect.

00:23:20.473 --> 00:23:24.170
But I use my experience to
try and make things work.

00:23:24.170 --> 00:23:25.840
Have you bought any furniture yet?

00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:29.010
Oh yeah, it's too late now.

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:31.420
Dr. Ngozi, thank you very much indeed

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:33.670
for your time and your experience.

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:34.990
Thank you, Emma.

00:23:34.990 --> 00:23:37.050
Thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:39.833
Until we meet again,
stay safe and goodbye.

